DATA = [{'filename': '25-tecnicas-cuidar-jardin.html', 'title_es': '25 técnicas para cuidar de tu jardín y convertir esta habilidad en una nueva oportunidad profesional', 'title_en': '25 Garden Care Techniques and How to Turn This Skill Into a Professional Opportunity', 'description': 'Discover 25 practical garden-care techniques to keep plants healthy, improve your outdoor space, and develop skills that may support professional garden-maintenance work.', 'intro': 'Gardening can be both a rewarding activity and a practical skill. These techniques will help you understand your plants, organize maintenance, and create healthier, more attractive outdoor spaces.', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>This guide brings together 25 practical garden-care techniques, explains how to organize them into a realistic routine, and shows how consistent gardening skills can become useful experience for paid garden-maintenance work.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Caring for a garden is not simply a matter of watering plants from time to time. A healthy outdoor space depends on observation, planning, suitable light, appropriate watering, soil conditions, seasonal changes, and regular maintenance. Every species responds differently, so the most useful habit is learning to look at the plant and its environment before deciding what it needs. The techniques below are designed for ordinary home gardens, patios, balconies, containers, and small planted areas. They are not complicated, but their value comes from applying them consistently. Start with the habits that fit your space, record what works, and adjust your routine as the garden changes through the year.</p>\n<h3>1. Observe your plants regularly</h3>\n<p>Spend a few minutes looking closely at leaves, stems, buds, flowers, and soil. Regular observation helps you notice changes early and understand what is normal for each plant. Instead of reacting immediately to every yellow leaf, compare the plant with its recent condition and consider changes in weather, watering, light, or location.</p>\n<h3>2. Understand the needs of each species</h3>\n<p>Plants that look similar can have very different requirements. Learn the preferred light, moisture, soil, temperature range, and mature size of the species you grow. A simple note on your phone or a small garden notebook can prevent you from treating every plant in exactly the same way.</p>\n<h3>3. Water according to actual conditions</h3>\n<p>Avoid watering automatically just because a particular day has arrived. Temperature, rainfall, wind, container size, soil, and season all influence how quickly moisture is lost. Check conditions first and adapt the amount and frequency rather than following a rigid schedule.</p>\n<h3>4. Check soil moisture before watering</h3>\n<p>The surface of the soil may look dry while deeper layers remain moist. Test below the surface before adding more water. This habit is especially useful with containers and can reduce both excessive watering and unnecessary stress caused by letting the root zone become too dry.</p>\n<h3>5. Provide the right amount of light</h3>\n<p>Observe how sunlight moves across the garden during the day. Some plants need direct sun, while others perform better with filtered light or partial shade. Positioning a plant according to its natural light requirements often solves problems that extra fertilizer or water cannot.</p>\n<h3>6. Improve soil gradually</h3>\n<p>Good soil supports root development, drainage, and access to nutrients. Pay attention to compaction, texture, drainage, and organic matter. Make changes according to the needs of the plants and the characteristics of the site instead of adding products without a clear reason.</p>\n<h3>7. Use containers with drainage</h3>\n<p>Pots should normally allow excess water to escape. Check that drainage holes are open and that containers are not sitting in accumulated water unless the species specifically requires very wet conditions. Suitable drainage makes moisture management much easier.</p>\n<h3>8. Keep garden tools clean</h3>\n<p>Remove soil and plant residue after using tools and store them in a dry, organized place. Clean tools are easier to work with and easier to inspect for damage. Cutting tools should also be kept appropriately sharp so that pruning cuts are more controlled.</p>\n<h3>9. Remove dry leaves and plant debris</h3>\n<p>Routine cleanup makes a garden look better and also makes inspection easier. Removing dead material lets you see the soil, stems, and new growth more clearly. Do not confuse normal seasonal leaf drop with a problem; learn what is typical for each species.</p>\n<h3>10. Control unwanted weeds early</h3>\n<p>Weeds and volunteer plants can compete for space, water, light, and nutrients. Removing them while they are small is generally easier than waiting until they are established. Regular light maintenance prevents a small task from becoming a major cleanup.</p>\n<h3>11. Prune with a clear purpose</h3>\n<p>Pruning should respond to the species, season, and a specific objective. Learn when and how a plant is normally pruned before cutting heavily. Removing dead or damaged material can be useful, but unnecessary pruning may weaken growth or spoil the natural shape.</p>\n<h3>12. Protect sensitive plants from extreme weather</h3>\n<p>Heat waves, cold periods, strong wind, and unusually heavy rain can stress plants. Pay closer attention during extreme conditions, particularly to young plants and containers. Temporary shade, shelter, or changes in watering may be appropriate depending on the species.</p>\n<h3>13. Manage aggressive growth</h3>\n<p>Some plants spread rapidly through roots, runners, or seed. Monitor them before they occupy space intended for other plants. Choosing a suitable location from the beginning and controlling unwanted spread early can save considerable maintenance later.</p>\n<h3>14. Watch for signs of pests</h3>\n<p>Inspect both sides of leaves, stems, buds, and new growth. Holes, sticky residue, distorted leaves, unusual spots, or visible insects deserve closer examination. Correct identification matters because different problems can produce similar symptoms and may require different responses.</p>\n<h3>15. Understand products before applying them</h3>\n<p>Read labels and follow instructions before using fertilizers or other garden products. More is not automatically better. Correct use protects plants, people, pets, and the surrounding environment, and it reduces the risk of damaging a plant through unnecessary treatment.</p>\n<h3>16. Fertilize according to need</h3>\n<p>Feeding requirements vary by plant, soil, season, and stage of growth. Avoid applying the same product and quantity to everything. When fertilizer is appropriate, use it as directed and observe the plant's response rather than trying to force rapid growth.</p>\n<h3>17. Create a simple maintenance routine</h3>\n<p>Divide garden care into small recurring tasks. For example, one day can be used for inspection, another for moisture checks, and another for cleaning or light pruning. Short, consistent sessions are often more effective than occasional hours of rushed maintenance.</p>\n<h3>18. Organize seasonal tasks</h3>\n<p>Some jobs are needed only at certain times of year. Keep a simple calendar for planting, pruning, repotting, soil improvement, weather protection, and other seasonal work. The calendar does not need to be complicated; it only needs to help you remember useful timing.</p>\n<h3>19. Use small spaces intelligently</h3>\n<p>A garden does not need to be large. Balconies, patios, terraces, windows, and compact yards can support attractive planting when species are chosen for the conditions available. Containers and vertical elements can add greenery while preserving circulation.</p>\n<h3>20. Choose plants suited to the local climate</h3>\n<p>A plant that is naturally comfortable with local temperatures and seasonal conditions is generally easier to maintain. Before buying a species only because it looks attractive, check whether it can realistically thrive in your region and in the microclimate of your garden.</p>\n<h3>21. Group plants with similar requirements</h3>\n<p>Plants with comparable light and water needs are easier to manage when placed near one another. This reduces conflicting watering routines in the same small bed and can also create a more coherent visual composition.</p>\n<h3>22. Keep the garden accessible</h3>\n<p>Do not allow furniture, stored objects, broken pots, or uncontrolled growth to block access to planted areas. Clear paths make watering, pruning, inspection, and cleaning easier and help the garden remain functional as well as attractive.</p>\n<h3>23. Record important changes</h3>\n<p>Write down major events such as repotting, planting, pruning, fertilizing, or moving a plant. Periodic photographs are useful too. Over time, these records help you identify patterns and understand which changes produced good results.</p>\n<h3>24. Learn from every season</h3>\n<p>Sun angles, temperatures, rainfall, growth rates, and flowering patterns change throughout the year. Notice these changes and adapt your routine instead of expecting the garden to behave exactly the same way every month.</p>\n<h3>25. Be patient and improve through practice</h3>\n<p>Gardening rewards consistency more than speed. Some results take weeks or months to become clear. Whenever possible, avoid changing several variables at the same time, because gradual adjustments make it easier to understand how a plant responds.</p>\n<h2>Turn Observation Into a Gardening Habit</h2>\n<p>Observation is one of the most valuable tools a gardener has. Compare each plant with its own normal appearance rather than relying only on photographs from other climates. Local humidity, light, temperature, soil, and watering patterns can create perfectly normal differences in size, color, and growth.</p>\n<p>When something changes, think about what happened recently. Was the plant moved? Did the weather become hotter? Has rainfall increased? Was it repotted or fed? Connecting visible changes with recent conditions helps you make more thoughtful decisions and prevents unnecessary interventions.</p>\n<h2>Organize Garden Work by Season</h2>\n<p>Not every task belongs in every month. Pruning, planting, feeding, repotting, and protection from extreme weather may have more suitable periods depending on the species and local climate. A seasonal plan makes maintenance more predictable and helps you prepare before busy periods.</p>\n<p>Keep records practical. Note only information that will help later, such as planting dates, important pruning, recurring problems, or when a container was last repotted. A simple system you actually use is more valuable than a detailed one you abandon.</p>\n<h2>Turning Gardening Skills Into Professional Work</h2>\n<p>Gardening knowledge can become the foundation for services such as routine residential garden maintenance, ornamental plant care, outdoor-space organization, seasonal cleanup, and basic planting. Practical experience, reliability, communication, and safe working habits are just as important as knowing plant names.</p>\n<p>Learning a list of techniques does not automatically make someone a professional. Clients depend on a gardener to understand the limits of a service, use tools responsibly, protect property, and recognize when a task requires specialist knowledge. Continued study and hands-on experience therefore remain important as responsibilities increase.</p>\n<h3>Services you can gradually develop</h3>\n<p>Possible services include routine garden inspections, care of ornamental containers, basic maintenance of planted areas, cleanup, appropriate light pruning within your skill level, and simple maintenance planning. Begin with work you can perform confidently and expand your services as your experience grows.</p>\n<p>Before offering paid work, check any rules that apply where you live and follow safety instructions for tools and products. Be transparent about your experience. Specialized tree work, electrical work, chemical application, or other regulated tasks may require training or professional authorization.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What is the most important garden-care technique?</h3>\n<p>There is no single technique for every garden. Regular observation and an understanding of each plant's light, water, soil, and seasonal requirements provide the strongest foundation for good decisions.</p>\n<h3>How long does it take to learn gardening?</h3>\n<p>Basic skills can be learned progressively, but experience develops over time. Different plants, soils, seasons, and spaces create new situations, so gardening remains an ongoing learning process.</p>\n<h3>Can gardening become paid work?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Routine garden maintenance can become a professional service, provided you develop practical experience, communicate clearly, work safely, and respect any local requirements that apply.</p>\n<h3>Do I need a large garden to practice?</h3>\n<p>No. Containers, balconies, patios, terraces, and small beds provide excellent opportunities to learn watering, observation, pruning, soil management, and seasonal care.</p>\n<h3>How can I improve faster?</h3>\n<p>Combine reliable reading with hands-on practice. Keep simple records, learn the species you grow, review your results, and expand your knowledge gradually instead of trying to master every topic at once.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Good garden care combines observation, knowledge, planning, and consistency. Plants do not all need the same treatment, so learning to recognize individual requirements is more useful than applying one routine to the entire garden.</p>\n<p>Use these 25 techniques as a practical foundation. Introduce them gradually, observe the results, and adapt your methods as weather and seasons change. With patience, every season becomes an opportunity to understand plants better and improve the space around them.</p>\n<p>If you decide to turn gardening into professional work, make that transition responsibly. Continue learning, use tools and products safely, respect local requirements, and offer only services you are prepared to perform well.</p>\n<h2>A Practical Decision-Making Routine</h2>\n<p>When you are unsure what to do in a garden, slow the decision down. Identify the plant, observe the site, check recent weather and maintenance, and change only what has a clear reason. This simple sequence reduces guesswork and makes it easier to understand the result of each action. Good gardening is often less about doing more and more about making better-timed decisions.</p>\n<p>Review the garden at regular intervals instead of waiting for obvious problems. A short weekly inspection can reveal changes in moisture, new growth, damaged material, crowding, or seasonal shifts. Keeping the routine simple makes it sustainable and gives you a much clearer picture of how the space develops over time.</p>\n<h2>Build Knowledge From Reliable Information</h2>\n<p>Plant labels are a starting point, not the entire story. When a species matters to your garden, compare information from reputable horticultural references and adapt it to local conditions. Pay particular attention to mature dimensions, climate tolerance, light, soil, water, and pruning. Understanding these fundamentals prevents many problems before they begin.</p>\n<p>Experience becomes more valuable when it is recorded. Take occasional photographs and note important changes. If a plant improves or declines, those records can help you connect the result with weather, watering, pruning, relocation, or another recent event. Over several seasons, your own garden becomes an excellent source of practical information.</p>", 'words': 2155}, {'filename': 'arboles-pequenos-para-jardines-domesticos.html', 'title_es': 'Árboles pequeños para jardines domésticos: cómo elegirlos y cuidarlos', 'title_en': 'Small Trees for Home Gardens: How to Choose and Care for Them', 'description': 'Learn how to choose small trees for home gardens based on mature size, available space, climate, sunlight, soil, design, and long-term maintenance.', 'intro': 'A small tree can bring structure, shade, seasonal interest, and character to a home garden. Choosing the right one means thinking about mature growth, location, climate, soil, and future maintenance before planting.', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>Choosing a small tree requires more than finding an attractive young specimen. This guide explains how to evaluate space, mature size, climate, soil, placement, maintenance, and design so that the tree can remain an asset to the garden for years.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A small tree can completely change a home garden. It can provide structure, seasonal interest, moderate shade, privacy, flowers, attractive foliage, or a focal point that makes the entire landscape feel more established. In a compact space, however, choosing the wrong tree can also create years of unnecessary pruning and conflicts with walls, paths, windows, and other plants.</p>\n<p>The key is to choose for the mature garden rather than for the nursery display. A young tree may occupy very little space today, but its crown and roots will develop over time. Before buying, compare the mature dimensions and growing requirements with the real conditions of your property.</p>\n<h2>Evaluate the available space</h2>\n<p>Measure more than the patch of soil where the trunk will stand. Look at overhead clearance, walls, windows, fences, paths, seating areas, utility lines, and neighboring plants. In a small garden, every meter matters, and the mature crown needs enough room to develop without becoming a permanent obstacle.</p>\n<h2>Think about mature size</h2>\n<p>Height is only part of the picture. Crown width, branching habit, and root development also matter. A tree can remain relatively short and still spread widely. Check reliable information about mature dimensions and remember that local climate and growing conditions can influence final size.</p>\n<h2>Define the purpose of the tree</h2>\n<p>Decide what you want the tree to contribute. Is the priority light shade, flowers, privacy, seasonal color, wildlife value, screening, or a strong focal point? A clear purpose makes it easier to compare suitable species instead of choosing only by appearance.</p>\n<h2>Study sunlight throughout the day</h2>\n<p>Observe the proposed location in the morning, midday, and afternoon. Buildings, walls, fences, and existing plants can create changing patterns of sun and shade. Match the tree's requirements to the actual exposure rather than assuming the entire garden receives the same light.</p>\n<h2>Consider the garden's microclimate</h2>\n<p>A sheltered corner can behave differently from an exposed lawn. Walls may reflect heat, paved areas can become warmer, and wind can dry foliage and soil more quickly. These small environmental differences influence which tree is most likely to thrive in a particular position.</p>\n<h2>Check climate suitability</h2>\n<p>Choose a species or cultivar that can handle the temperatures and seasonal conditions typical of your region. A beautiful tree that struggles with local heat, cold, humidity, or rainfall may demand constant intervention and still perform poorly.</p>\n<h2>Understand the soil</h2>\n<p>Soil texture, drainage, compaction, and available rooting space influence establishment. Observe what happens after rain and consider whether the soil stays saturated or dries very quickly. A suitable species matched to the existing conditions is often easier to maintain than one that requires continual correction.</p>\n<h2>Prepare the planting area</h2>\n<p>Remove major debris and make sure the site is appropriate before planting. The goal is to allow roots to establish into the surrounding soil rather than remain confined to a small pocket. Follow suitable planting guidance for the species and local soil conditions.</p>\n<h2>Leave appropriate distance from structures</h2>\n<p>Think ahead to the mature crown and roots. Give the tree reasonable separation from walls, fences, paving, doors, windows, and other permanent features. Appropriate spacing improves access and reduces the likelihood that repeated corrective pruning will be needed later.</p>\n<h2>Consider underground services</h2>\n<p>What is below the ground matters as much as what is visible above it. Pipes, drainage systems, utilities, foundations, and other infrastructure can affect where planting is appropriate. When you are uncertain, obtain reliable information before digging.</p>\n<h2>Plan for establishment watering</h2>\n<p>Newly planted trees generally need closer moisture monitoring while roots establish. The exact schedule depends on species, weather, soil, rainfall, and season. Check actual soil conditions rather than assuming that every new tree needs the same amount of water.</p>\n<h2>Use mulch appropriately</h2>\n<p>An appropriate mulch layer can help reduce moisture loss and moderate soil conditions. Keep material away from direct contact with the trunk instead of piling it against the base. The objective is to support the root zone without creating a permanently damp collar around the trunk.</p>\n<h2>Prune with a purpose</h2>\n<p>Pruning should respond to a clear need and the natural growth habit of the tree. Removing damaged or problematic growth can be useful, but repeatedly cutting a tree because it is too large for its location indicates that the original selection or placement may have been unsuitable.</p>\n<h2>Inspect the tree regularly</h2>\n<p>Look at foliage, branches, new growth, the trunk, and soil conditions. Inspection after unusual heat, strong wind, heavy rain, or other stressful weather can be especially useful. Early observation makes it easier to notice changes and seek appropriate advice when necessary.</p>\n<h2>Think about seasonal appearance</h2>\n<p>Some trees are valued for spring flowers, others for autumn foliage, fruit, bark, or an attractive winter structure. Consider how the tree will contribute throughout the year rather than choosing only for the season in which you happen to visit the nursery.</p>\n<h2>Plan for leaf and fruit drop</h2>\n<p>Natural debris is part of living with a tree. Falling leaves or fruit may be perfectly acceptable in a planting bed but less convenient over a frequently used patio, pool area, or narrow path. Position the tree with routine cleanup in mind.</p>\n<h2>Use the tree as a focal point</h2>\n<p>A small tree can visually organize a garden. It may anchor a planting bed, frame a view from a window, or mark the transition between a patio and planted area. Give it enough visual space so that its form can be appreciated rather than crowding it among too many competing elements.</p>\n<h2>Combine it with compatible plants</h2>\n<p>Plants beneath and around a tree should tolerate the conditions that will exist as the crown matures. Shade may increase over time, and roots will occupy more of the surrounding soil. Select companions with compatible light and moisture requirements and leave room for development.</p>\n<h2>Avoid overcrowding</h2>\n<p>A newly planted garden can look sparse, which tempts people to fill every gap. Remember that trees, shrubs, and perennials grow. Allowing appropriate spacing from the beginning can produce a more balanced garden later and reduce unnecessary transplanting.</p>\n<h2>Choose a healthy nursery specimen</h2>\n<p>Look beyond flowers or attractive leaves. Examine the overall structure, visible trunk, foliage, and general condition. Ask about mature height and width, growth rate, climate suitability, sunlight, soil, and care. If several cultivars exist, compare their mature characteristics carefully.</p>\n<h2>Take measurements when shopping</h2>\n<p>Bring your garden measurements with you. Knowing the maximum practical crown width and height helps you resist buying a tree that is attractive but unsuitable. In a compact garden, even a modest difference in mature size can have a major impact.</p>\n<h2>Sketch very small gardens first</h2>\n<p>For a tiny yard or patio garden, make a simple plan showing doors, windows, paths, furniture, beds, and permanent structures. Draw the approximate mature crown rather than the current nursery size. This quickly reveals conflicts that can be difficult to imagine in the store.</p>\n<h2>Consider views from inside the house</h2>\n<p>A well-positioned tree can improve the view from a living room, bedroom, or kitchen and provide seasonal interest even when you are indoors. At the same time, avoid placing it where mature growth will unnecessarily block valuable natural light.</p>\n<h2>Keep maintenance access</h2>\n<p>Leave enough room to reach the tree for watering, inspection, cleanup, and pruning. A tree squeezed behind furniture or against a fence may technically fit but become frustrating to care for. Practical access is part of good garden design.</p>\n<h2>Give the tree time to establish</h2>\n<p>Do not expect immediate mature growth. Establishment depends on species, season, weather, soil, and the condition of the plant. Consistent basic care and patient observation are usually more useful than repeatedly changing treatments without a clear reason.</p>\n<h2>Designing Around a Small Tree</h2>\n<p>Once the tree has a suitable position, use it to guide the rest of the composition. Lower plants can create a transition around the trunk area, while shrubs and groundcovers can connect the tree visually with nearby beds. Repeating a few compatible textures and forms often makes a small garden feel more coherent than filling it with many unrelated species.</p>\n<p>Restraint is especially valuable when the tree is intended to be the main feature. Leave some visual breathing room and allow the garden to develop. You can add plants later after observing how the tree changes shade, moisture, and circulation around it.</p>\n<h2>Small Trees in Containers</h2>\n<p>Some species and compact cultivars can be grown in sufficiently large containers, but container culture changes the way roots experience moisture and temperature. Drainage, root space, watering, container stability, and long-term size become especially important. Research the particular tree rather than assuming any small species will remain comfortable in a pot indefinitely.</p>\n<p>Containers can be useful where planting in the ground is impossible, and they offer design flexibility on patios and terraces. They also require closer observation because the root zone can dry or heat more quickly than surrounding garden soil. Plan for the eventual weight and size of the container before positioning it.</p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>One of the most common mistakes is selecting from a photograph or from the appearance of a young nursery specimen without checking mature dimensions. Another is planting too close to buildings or paths and expecting frequent pruning to solve the problem permanently.</p>\n<p>It is also easy to ignore maintenance. Even compact trees require some combination of establishment watering, inspection, seasonal cleanup, and appropriate pruning. Choosing a tree that naturally suits the space is one of the most effective ways to keep that maintenance reasonable.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid treating every small tree as interchangeable. Climate, soil, sunlight, drainage, and mature form matter. A species that performs beautifully in one garden may be poorly suited to another property only a short distance away because the microclimate is different.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How large should a tree be for a small garden?</h3>\n<p>There is no single ideal measurement. Compare mature height and crown width with the available space, then consider roots, nearby structures, access, and the function you want the tree to perform.</p>\n<h3>Is a slow-growing tree always better?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily. Moderate growth can be convenient, but suitability for climate, soil, sunlight, mature size, and available space is more important than growth speed alone.</p>\n<h3>Where should I plant a small tree?</h3>\n<p>Choose a location with suitable light and soil where the mature tree has room without interfering with paths, doors, windows, walls, utilities, or important garden activities.</p>\n<h3>Can a small tree grow in a container?</h3>\n<p>Some species and cultivars can, but container size, drainage, root space, watering, temperature, and mature growth need careful consideration. Research the specific tree before choosing this approach.</p>\n<h3>How much maintenance does a small tree need?</h3>\n<p>Maintenance varies by species and site. Expect closer attention during establishment, periodic inspection, cleanup, and pruning when appropriate. Good initial selection greatly reduces unnecessary work.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Choosing a small tree for a home garden is a long-term decision. The best option is not necessarily the most dramatic specimen at the nursery, but the tree whose mature size, climate needs, light requirements, soil preferences, and maintenance fit the real conditions of the property.</p>\n<p>Measure first, study the site, define what you want the tree to contribute, and think several years ahead. Good planning allows a tree to develop more naturally and reduces conflicts with buildings, paths, other plants, and everyday use of the garden.</p>\n<p>With thoughtful selection and consistent care, a small tree can become one of the most valuable elements in a compact landscape, providing structure, shade, seasonal character, and a sense of maturity without overwhelming the available space.</p>\n<h2>A Practical Decision-Making Routine</h2>\n<p>When you are unsure what to do in a garden, slow the decision down. Identify the plant, observe the site, check recent weather and maintenance, and change only what has a clear reason. This simple sequence reduces guesswork and makes it easier to understand the result of each action. Good gardening is often less about doing more and more about making better-timed decisions.</p>\n<p>Review the garden at regular intervals instead of waiting for obvious problems. A short weekly inspection can reveal changes in moisture, new growth, damaged material, crowding, or seasonal shifts. Keeping the routine simple makes it sustainable and gives you a much clearer picture of how the space develops over time.</p>\n<h2>Build Knowledge From Reliable Information</h2>\n<p>Plant labels are a starting point, not the entire story. When a species matters to your garden, compare information from reputable horticultural references and adapt it to local conditions. Pay particular attention to mature dimensions, climate tolerance, light, soil, water, and pruning. Understanding these fundamentals prevents many problems before they begin.</p>\n<p>Experience becomes more valuable when it is recorded. Take occasional photographs and note important changes. If a plant improves or declines, those records can help you connect the result with weather, watering, pruning, relocation, or another recent event. Over several seasons, your own garden becomes an excellent source of practical information.</p>", 'words': 2177}]

def count_words(html):
    text=re.sub(r"<[^>]+>"," ",html)
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b",text))

print("="*68)
print("BLOCK 03 - ARTICLES 01 AND 02")
print("="*68)

for d in DATA:
    words=count_words(d["body"])
    if not 2000 <= words <= 3000:
        print(f'ERROR - {d["filename"]}: {words} words')
        raise SystemExit(1)
    p=ART/d["filename"]
    if not p.exists():
        print("ERROR - article not found:",p)
        raise SystemExit(1)

for d in DATA:
    p=ART/d["filename"]
    text=p.read_text(encoding="utf-8",errors="replace")
    backup=BACK/d["filename"]
    if not backup.exists(): shutil.copy2(p,backup)

    text=re.sub(r'<html\s+lang=["\']es["\']','<html lang="en"',text,flags=re.I)
    text=text.replace(d["title_es"],d["title_en"])
    text=re.sub(r'(<meta[^>]+name=["\']description["\'][^>]+content=["\'])[^"\']*(["\'])',
                lambda m:m.group(1)+d["description"]+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    text=re.sub(r'(<p[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-intro[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</p>)',
                lambda m:m.group(1)+d["intro"]+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    text=re.sub(r'(<span[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-category[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</span>)',
                lambda m:m.group(1)+"GARDENING"+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    pat=r'(<article\b[^>]*class=["\'][^"\']*article-content-full[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</article>)'
    text,n=re.subn(pat,lambda m:m.group(1)+"\n"+d["body"]+"\n"+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    if n != 1:
        print("ERROR - article-content-full not found:",d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

    common={"Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden","Publicado:":"Published:","Actualizado:":"Updated:",
            "Inicio":"Home","Categorías":"Categories","Sobre nosotros":"About Us",
            "Contacto":"Contact","Compartir":"Share","Artículos recientes":"Recent Articles",
            "También te puede interesar":"You May Also Like"}
    for old,new in common.items(): text=text.replace(old,new)
    p.write_text(text,encoding="utf-8")
    print(f'OK - {d["filename"]} -> {count_words(d["body"])} words')

print()
print("ARTICLES 01-02: APPROVED")
